From 059e4026fcac92de8d1a479d7a3fc066d9eecd0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shammah Chancellor <shammah.chancellor@notvalid.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 14:50:31 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fixup! http: delegate /chronik/* and /chronik/ws on main RPC
 port to Chronik server

Serve /chronik in process instead of through a loopback proxy

Forwarding each /chronik request and /chronik/ws session over a new
loopback connection to Chronik's own listener made Chronik see every
client as 127.0.0.1 (or ::1): the per-IP WebSocket subscription limit
(-chronikmaxsubsperip) put all of them in one budget, so one client
could exhaust it for everyone.

The HTTP server instead takes in-process handlers for /chronik,
ChronikInProcessHandlers in httpserver.h (no Chronik dependency):

- http(req, done): method, target without the /chronik prefix,
  headers, body and the client address of the HTTP server's socket;
  done(response) is called once, from any thread.
- ws_check(req): whether an upgrade is a valid /ws upgrade, and the
  extra headers of the 101 response.
- ws_open(ws, peer_ip): the session handler. The HTTP server does the
  WebSocket framing, TLS, size limit and pings (ws_ping_interval,
  ws_ping_payload); the handler gets messages in order and OnClosed
  once. InProcessWebSocket::SendBinary/Close are thread-safe.

TLS, zstd, the response size cap and the -rpcallowip exemption of the
public prefix stay the HTTP server's. Without registered handlers
(before Chronik starts, after it is interrupted) /chronik answers 503.
X-Forwarded-For is never read for Chronik.

Rust Chronik registers through its cxx bridge (chronik-cpp/
chronik_inprocess.cpp, chronik-lib in_process.rs):

- HTTP requests are served by the same axum Router as -chronikbind,
  called as a tower Service on Chronik's runtime, with the client
  address as the ConnectInfo extension.
- WebSocket sessions run the same subscription loop as -chronikbind,
  handle_subscribe_socket, over a WsTransport: Axum (Chronik's own
  listener; Chronik sends the pings) or InProcess (client messages
  through a channel of up to 1024 queued messages, a client further
  ahead is disconnected; Chronik's messages to the HTTP server's
  session).
- Both use one subscription limiter, keyed on the real client address:
  a client is counted once, by its address, whichever way it connects.
- chronik::Interrupt unregisters the handlers, closing the sessions,
  before Chronik's runtime shuts down; requests still pending then get
  503.

-chronikbind keeps serving direct clients with their TCP peer address.

chronik_rpc_port_proxy.py checks that 127.0.0.1 and ::1 clients on the
RPC port get separate subscription budgets, that a forged
X-Forwarded-For is ignored on both paths, and that a direct client
shares the budget of its address.

Tier: eCash (fixup to ecash/075)
